$ mp --help
A terminal email client: Markdown drafts on disk, received mail in a local store

Usage: mp [OPTIONS] [SELECTOR] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  send            Send a single approved email, or (with --invite) a calendar invitation
  send-approved   Send every approved draft of the account
  list            List the account's drafts from the drafts index
  validate        Validate a draft's frontmatter (default: every draft of the account)
  mark-approved   Mark a draft as approved
  mark-draft      Demote an approved draft back to `draft` status (reverse of `mark-approved`)
  new             Create a new email draft from template and print its selector
  path            Print the filesystem path of a draft (the only selector-to-path edge)
  edit            Open a draft in $EDITOR
  reply           Create a reply draft from a received email
  forward         Forward an email to new recipients
  invite          Answer a received calendar invitation: through the server when the account's server calendar holds its event, else by iMIP reply
  list-mailboxes  List every folder on the server with its sync policy and role
  sync            Sync mailboxes from the server into the local store
  watch           Watch a mailbox for changes using IMAP IDLE
  archive         Archive received emails (server + local)
  move            Move received emails to another mailbox (server + local)
  block           Block a sender: move its mail from every Inbox to Junk, now and as it arrives
  unblock         Remove a sender from the block list; nothing moves back from Junk
  blocked         List the blocked senders
  delete          Delete received emails (server + local) or local drafts
  open            Open a received email's attachment in the default application
  save            Save a received email's attachment(s) to a directory
  export          Write a received email's raw RFC 822 message to <date>_<subject>.eml
  show            Print one received message from the local store, fetched first if missing
  list-messages   List received messages from the local store (offline)
  search          Search emails on the mail server (IMAP or Exchange), or locally with --local
  config          Manage configuration
  contacts        Contact index operations
  signature       Manage the signatures drafts carry, and each account's default
  calendar        The calendar: list, view, show and hide calendars, free/busy, reminders, rebuild
  event           One calendar event: create, change, cancel, delete, show or answer it
  hooks           Inspect, dry-run and replay the mail hooks of `[[accounts.hooks]]`
  outbox          Inspect and unblock the durable send queue
  store           Local store maintenance (retention garbage collection)
  cutover         Report what is left of the file-era `.md` tree, and import its drafts
  dump-keys       Dump the TUI key bindings from the single KEYMAP source of truth
  dump-mailbox    Dump message envelopes from the local message store as NDJSON
  completions     Print a shell completion script (eval or save under the shell's completion dir)
  account         Inspect the configured accounts
  daemon          Manage the local mailypoppins daemon (run, start, status, stop, restart)
  help            Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]  Draft selector to preview (dry-run mode)

Options:
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>  Account to use
      --profile <NAME>     Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help               Print help
  -V, --version            Print version

$ mp send --help
Send a single approved email, or (with --invite) a calendar invitation

Usage: mp send [OPTIONS] [SELECTOR]

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]  Draft selector: mp://<account>/drafts/<id>, drafts/<id> or <id> (omit when using --invite)

Options:
  -y, --yes                        Skip confirmation prompt
      --invite                     Send an iMIP calendar invitation (METHOD:REQUEST) instead of a draft. Attendees come from --to/--cc; the subject is used as the event summary. Requires --to, --start and --subject; a timed event also needs one of --end/--duration, an all-day event (a bare date --start) does not
      --to <TO>                    Invite recipient(s), comma-separated (invite mode; ATTENDEE + To)
      --cc <CC>                    Invite CC recipient(s), comma-separated (invite mode; ATTENDEE + Cc)
      --subject <SUBJECT>          Event subject / summary (invite mode)
      --start <START>              Event start. Local time (2026-07-20T14:00 or "2026-07-20 14:00") or RFC3339 with offset (2026-07-20T14:00:00+02:00, ...Z). A bare date (2026-12-24) makes an all-day event. Invite mode
      --end <END>                  Event end, the same kind as --start. For an all-day event it is the last day, inclusive (--start 2026-12-24 --end 2027-01-08 covers 24 Dec through 8 Jan); omit it for a single day. A timed event needs this or --duration
      --duration <DURATION>        Event duration instead of --end: ISO8601 (PT1H30M) or short (1h30m); whole days (P3D, 3d) for an all-day event
      --location <LOCATION>        Optional event location (invite mode)
      --description <DESCRIPTION>  Optional event description / body (invite mode)
      --uid <UID>                  Re-send an invitation already sent, as an update: give its UID (the `UID:` line of its preview or its invite.ics) and a higher --sequence. Calendar clients replace the event only when the UID is the same and the SEQUENCE is higher than the one they hold; without --uid a fresh UID is minted (a new event)
      --sequence <N>               The update's SEQUENCE, higher than the last one sent for this UID (the first invitation goes out as 0, so a first update is 1). Needs --uid
  -s, --signature <SIGNATURE>      Signature to use in the invitation (overrides config default)
      --no-signature               Skip the signature in the invitation
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>          Account to use
      --profile <NAME>             Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help                       Print help

