$ mp block --help
Block a sender: move its mail from every Inbox to Junk, now and as it arrives

Usage: mp block [OPTIONS] <ADDRESS>

Arguments:
  <ADDRESS>  A full address (spam@example.com) or a domain (@example.com)

Options:
      --keep-existing      Leave the sender's mail already in the Inbox; only new mail moves
      --json               Emit the daemon's answer as one JSON object {entry, added, entries, moved: [{account, mailbox, count}], skipped: [{account, reason}]}
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>  Account to use
      --profile <NAME>     Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help               Print help

The daemon keeps one block list for every account. A sender is a full address
or a domain written @example.com, which leaves its subdomains out, matched
against the From address in any case. Blocking moves the sender's mail in each
account's Inbox to that account's Junk folder, on the server too, and after
every sync the daemon moves what arrived from a blocked sender since. A message
moved back to the Inbox stays there. An account with no Junk folder keeps the
mail, and the command says so. Nothing is set on the server, so mail moves only
while the daemon runs. An account's own address (its default_from) and its
domain are refused, since blocking them would move your own mail to Junk.

Examples:
  mp block offers@shop.example
  mp block @spam.example
  mp block @spam.example --keep-existing

$ mp unblock --help
Remove a sender from the block list; nothing moves back from Junk

Usage: mp unblock [OPTIONS] <ADDRESS>

Arguments:
  <ADDRESS>  The address or @domain as it was blocked, in any case

Options:
      --json               Emit the daemon's answer as one JSON object {entry, removed, entries}
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>  Account to use
      --profile <NAME>     Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help               Print help

