Report what the stored attendee REPLY emails resolve on the stored invitations, and resync the server calendar of an account that has one. The fold writes nothing: attendee statuses are derived from the `invite.ics` payloads wherever they are displayed, so there is no cached copy to rebuild. Covers every configured account unless `-A` names one

Usage: mp calendar rebuild [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>  Account to use
      --profile <NAME>     Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help               Print help

$ mp event --help
One calendar event: create, change, cancel, delete, show or answer it

Usage: mp event [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  create   Create an event in the account's server calendar; the server sends the invitations
  update   Change an event: only the fields you name change, and the server sends the attendees an update
  cancel   Cancel a meeting you organize: every attendee gets the cancellation
  delete   Delete an event that tells nobody: one without attendees, or your copy of a meeting its organizer cancelled
  show     Show one event: when, where, organizer, attendees, your answer
  respond  Answer an invitation: through the server for a server event, by iMIP reply for an invitation mail's event
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>  Account to use
      --profile <NAME>     Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help               Print help

Examples:
  mp event create -A evoqs --summary "Project review" --start 2026-11-18T14:00 --duration 1h --to ada@example.com --teams
  mp event create -A evoqs --summary Away --start 2026-12-24 --end 2026-12-31 --no-reminder
  mp event show AAMkADev2 -A evoqs
  mp event respond AAMkADev2 accept --comment "See you there" -A evoqs
  mp event respond imip:42 decline -A perso

$ mp event create --help
Create an event in the account's server calendar; the server sends the invitations

Usage: mp event create [OPTIONS] --summary <TEXT> --start <WHEN>

Options:
      --calendar <ID>        The calendar id, as `mp calendar list` prints it (default the account's default calendar)
      --summary <TEXT>       The title
      --start <WHEN>         When the event starts
      --end <WHEN>           When it ends; on an all-day event the last day, included
      --duration <D>         How long it lasts (1h30m, PT1H30M; whole days on an all-day event)
      --all-day              An all-day event: --start and --end are bare dates
      --zone <ZONE>          The IANA zone the event is held in (default the local zone)
      --location <TEXT>      Where it takes place
      --body <TEXT>          The description
      --body-file <PATH>     The description, read from a file (- for stdin)
      --to <ADDR>            A required attendee (repeatable)
      --optional <ADDR>      An optional attendee (repeatable)
      --room <ADDR>          A room mailbox (repeatable)
      --teams                Add a Teams meeting link (when the calendar offers Teams)
      --reminder <MIN>       Minutes before the start the reminder fires (default the mailbox's)
      --no-reminder          No reminder
      --transaction-id <ID>  The id of an earlier run of this create, which the server makes once
      --repeat <FREQ>        Repeat the event as a series [possible values: daily, weekly, monthly, yearly]
      --every <N>            Every N days, weeks, months or years (default 1)
      --on <DAYS>            The days of a weekly series, or the weekday of a monthly one by weekday (MO,WE; default the start's)
      --monthly-by <BY>      What a monthly series falls on: the start's day of the month (date, the default) or its weekday in the same week of the month [possible values: date, weekday]
      --until <DATE>         The series' last day (YYYY-MM-DD)
      --count <N>            How many occurrences the series has
      --json                 Print the settled `event.create` result as JSON on stdout
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>    Account to use
      --profile <NAME>       Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help                 Print help

WHEN is a time read in --zone (2026-11-18T14:00), a time with an offset
(2026-11-18T14:00:00+01:00), or a bare date (2026-11-18) for an all-day event,
whose --end is its last day, included. --duration takes 1h30m or PT1H30M.

--repeat makes a series: --every N for every N days, weeks, months or years,
--on MO,WE for the days of a weekly series (default the start's), --monthly-by
weekday for a monthly series on the start's weekday in the same week of the
month (default its day of the month), and --until DATE or --count N for its
end (default none). A yearly series falls on the start's day and month.

The command prints the create's transaction id when it cannot confirm the
event; rerun it with --transaction-id and the server creates the event at
most once.

Examples:
  mp event create -A evoqs --summary Standup --start 2026-11-23T09:00 --duration 15m --repeat weekly --on MO,WE,FR --until 2026-12-18
  mp event create -A evoqs --summary Review --start 2026-11-26T14:00 --duration 1h --repeat monthly --monthly-by weekday --count 6

$ mp event update --help
Change an event: only the fields you name change, and the server sends the attendees an update

Usage: mp event update [OPTIONS] <EVENT>

Arguments:
  <EVENT>  The event id, as `mp calendar view --json` prints it

Options:
      --scope <SCOPE>        What a change to an occurrence of a series reaches: it alone, or the whole series [possible values: occurrence, series]
      --summary <TEXT>       The new title
      --start <WHEN>         The new start
      --end <WHEN>           The new end; on an all-day event the last day, included
      --duration <D>         The new length (1h30m, PT1H30M; whole days on an all-day event)
      --all-day              Make it an all-day event: --start and --end are bare dates
      --zone <ZONE>          The IANA zone the new times are read in (default the event's own)
      --location <TEXT>      Where it takes place
      --clear-location       Remove the location
      --body <TEXT>          The new description
      --body-file <PATH>     The new description, read from a file (- for stdin)
      --clear-body           Remove the description
      --to <ADDR>            A required attendee of the new list (repeatable)
      --optional <ADDR>      An optional attendee of the new list (repeatable)
      --room <ADDR>          A room mailbox of the new list (repeatable)
      --teams                Add a Teams meeting link (when the calendar offers Teams); a link once added stays
      --reminder <MIN>       Minutes before the start the reminder fires
      --no-reminder          No reminder
      --show-as <WORD>       How the time shows to others: free, tentative, busy, oof, working-elsewhere
      --repeat <FREQ>        Repeat the event as a series [possible values: daily, weekly, monthly, yearly]
      --every <N>            Every N days, weeks, months or years (default 1)
      --on <DAYS>            The days of a weekly series, or the weekday of a monthly one by weekday (MO,WE; default the start's)
      --monthly-by <BY>      What a monthly series falls on: the start's day of the month (date, the default) or its weekday in the same week of the month [possible values: date, weekday]
      --until <DATE>         The series' last day (YYYY-MM-DD)
      --count <N>            How many occurrences the series has
      --use-proposal <ADDR>  Move the meeting to the time the attendee ADDR proposed, as `mp event show` lists it
      --json                 Print the settled `event.update` result as JSON on stdout
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>    Account to use
      --profile <NAME>       Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help                 Print help

WHEN takes the forms of mp event create. A new --start keeps the event's
length unless --end or --duration names a new one, and an --end or a
--duration alone keeps its start; the times are read in the event's own zone
unless --zone names another. --to, --optional and --room name the whole new
attendee list. An attendee may change only --reminder, --no-reminder and
--show-as on their own copy of a meeting; the rest is the organizer's.
--use-proposal moves the meeting to the time an attendee proposed, which
mp event show lists under the attendee: on an occurrence of a series it
moves that occurrence alone, since the attendee proposed for that one
meeting, and on a series master the whole series.

An occurrence of a series needs --scope: occurrence changes it alone, series
changes the whole series, its times read against the series' first
occurrence; a series' new time or --repeat resets the occurrences changed on
their own. --repeat takes the flags of mp event create, with --scope series.

Examples:
  mp event update AAMkADev1 --start 2026-11-18T15:00 -A evoqs
  mp event update AAMkADev1 --summary 'Budget, final' --clear-location -A evoqs
  mp event update AAMkADocc --scope series --summary 'Standup, new room' -A evoqs
  mp event update AAMkADev1 --use-proposal ada@example.com -A evoqs

$ mp event cancel --help
Cancel a meeting you organize: every attendee gets the cancellation

Usage: mp event cancel [OPTIONS] <EVENT>

Arguments:
  <EVENT>  The event id, as `mp calendar view --json` prints it

Options:
      --scope <SCOPE>      What a cancel of an occurrence of a series reaches [possible values: occurrence, series]
      --comment <TEXT>     A message for the attendees, sent with the cancellation
      --json               Print the settled `event.cancel` result as JSON on stdout
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>  Account to use
      --profile <NAME>     Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help               Print help

A meeting that invites nobody is deleted with mp event delete, and a meeting
someone else organizes is declined with mp event respond EVENT decline. An
occurrence of a series needs --scope: occurrence cancels it alone, series
cancels the whole series.

$ mp event delete --help
Delete an event that tells nobody: one without attendees, or your copy of a meeting its organizer cancelled

Usage: mp event delete [OPTIONS] <EVENT>

Arguments:
  <EVENT>  The event id, as `mp calendar view --json` prints it

Options:
      --scope <SCOPE>      What a delete of an occurrence of a series reaches [possible values: occurrence, series]
      --json               Print the settled `event.delete` result as JSON on stdout
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>  Account to use
      --profile <NAME>     Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help               Print help

A live meeting is not deleted: its organizer cancels it (mp event cancel), so
the attendees are told, and an attendee declines it (mp event respond EVENT
decline), so the organizer is. An occurrence of a series needs --scope:
occurrence deletes it alone, series deletes the whole series.

$ mp event show --help
Show one event: when, where, organizer, attendees, your answer

Usage: mp event show [OPTIONS] <EVENT>

Arguments:
  <EVENT>  The event id: a server id, or imip:<row_id> for an invitation mail's event

Options:
      --json               Print the `event.get` answer as JSON on stdout
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>  Account to use
      --profile <NAME>     Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help               Print help

$ mp event respond --help
Answer an invitation: through the server for a server event, by iMIP reply for an invitation mail's event

Usage: mp event respond [OPTIONS] <EVENT> <RESPONSE>

Arguments:
  <EVENT>     The event id: a server id, or imip:<row_id> for an invitation mail's event
  <RESPONSE>  The answer [possible values: accept, tentative, decline]

Options:
      --comment <TEXT>        A comment for the organizer
      --no-send-response      Record the answer without telling the organizer, and so without a comment (server events only: an iMIP reply is the answer itself)
      --propose <WHEN>        Propose a new start to the organizer (tentative or decline)
      --propose-end <WHEN>    The proposed end (default: the event's length)
      --propose-duration <D>  The proposed length (1h30m, PT1H30M)
      --zone <ZONE>           The IANA zone the proposed times are read in (default the event's own, the local zone for an all-day event)
      --json                  Print the settled `event.respond` result as JSON on stdout
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>     Account to use
      --profile <NAME>        Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help                  Print help

A tentative or a decline of a server event may propose a new time to the
organizer: --propose takes the forms of mp event create's WHEN, read in the
event's own zone (the local zone for an all-day event) unless --zone names
another, and the proposal keeps the event's length unless --propose-end or
--propose-duration names another; a bare date alone on a timed meeting
proposes that whole day. The organizer sees it on the event and takes it
with mp event update EVENT --use-proposal ADDR. A proposal must reach the
organizer, so it is refused with --no-send-response, as a --comment is, and
an invitation mail's event (imip:<row_id>) takes none: mp builds no
iCalendar COUNTER.

Example:
  mp event respond AAMkADev1 tentative --propose 2026-11-18T15:00 -A evoqs

$ mp hooks --help
Inspect, dry-run and replay the mail hooks of `[[accounts.hooks]]`

Usage: mp hooks [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  list    List the hooks with their cursor and last run (every configured account unless `-A` names one)
  test    Check a hook against a stored message; runs nothing, moves no cursor
  replay  Run a hook for a stored message now, whatever its cursor says (the `match` table still has to pass)
  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>  Account to use
      --profile <NAME>     Profile to run on, with its own config, data and daemon (default: $MAILYPOPPINS_PROFILE, else the default profile)
  -h, --help               Print help

The daemon runs a hook's command once for every message that arrives after
the hook was first seen and passes its `match` table. See
docs/daemon-operations.md for the configuration and the JSON on stdin.

Examples:
  mp hooks list
  mp hooks test pi-remote 'mp://assistant/inbox/abc@example.com'
  mp hooks replay pi-remote 'mp://assistant/inbox/abc@example.com'

$ mp hooks list --help
